{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness, specifically defining a lottery as right-skewed when a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes (i.e., the 0 Euro outcome) compared to favorable outcomes (i.e., the positive payoff). In this decision problem, both lotteries have a non-zero probability of yielding 0 Euros (67% for A, 66% for B), and a positive payoff (2500€ for A, 2400€ for B). The unfavorable outcome (0 Euros) dominates in both cases. However, the reason defines right-skewness as having a greater portion of probability on unfavorable outcomes. Since both lotteries have the majority of probability (over 66%) on the 0 Euro outcome, both are right-skewed. The key difference lies in the magnitude of the unfavorable probability: A has 67% and B has 66%, so A has a slightly higher probability of the unfavorable outcome. Thus, the decision reason would prefer A because it is more right-skewed (67% vs. 66% on 0 Euros). The relevant information for the reason is therefore the probability mass on the 0 Euro outcome (unfavorable) and the positive payoff (favorable). This information is fully present in the lotteries' structure and can be derived.",
  "step_2": "The individual's verbal report states: 'Die Wahrscheinlichkeit um 1% zu erhöhen, aber dafür 100€ weniger zu bekommen, macht meiner Meinung nach keinen Sinn.' This translates to: 'Increasing the probability by 1% but having to lose 100€, in my opinion, doesn't make sense.' This reflects a comparison between the two lotteries: Lottery A has a 33% chance of 2500€ and 67% chance of 0€, while Lottery B has 34% chance of 2400€ and 66% chance of 0€. The individual perceives a trade-off: a 1% increase in probability (from 33% to 34%) at the cost of a 100€ reduction in payoff (from 2500€ to 2400€). The individual dismisses this trade-off as irrational. This suggests that the individual values the certainty of a higher payoff more than a small increase in probability. The individual does not mention skewness, the distribution of outcomes, or the relative size of the unfavorable probability mass. The focus is on the monetary trade-off, not on the asymmetry or skewness of the outcomes. Therefore, the individual's deliberation centers on expected value or risk aversion, not on the skewness of the lottery distributions.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason is based on skewness — specifically, preferring the lottery with a greater probability mass on unfavorable outcomes (i.e., 0 Euros). In this case, Lottery A has a 67% chance of 0 Euros, while Lottery B has 66%, so A is more right-skewed. The individual, however, does not mention skewness or the distribution of probabilities across outcomes. Instead, the individual evaluates a trade-off between a 1% increase in probability and a 100€ decrease in payoff, dismissing it as irrational. This indicates a preference for higher expected value or a risk-averse stance, not a focus on the skewness of the outcomes. There is no reference to the unfavorable outcome being more probable or to the structure of the distribution. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based decision reason. The information relevant to the reason (probability on unfavorable outcomes) is present, but not considered or discussed in the justification. The justification is based on a different principle (monetary trade-off), not on skewness. Hence, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness, specifically defining right-skewness as a greater portion of probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes. In this decision problem, Lottery A has a 80% chance of 4000€ and a 20% chance of 0€. This means the bulk of the probability (80%) is on a favorable outcome (4000€), and only 20% is on an unfavorable outcome (0€). Thus, Lottery A is left-skewed (more probability on favorable outcomes). Lottery B offers 3000€ with 100% probability — a certain outcome, so it has no unfavorable outcomes and is symmetric or non-skewed. Therefore, neither lottery is right-skewed; in fact, A is left-skewed and B is not skewed right at all. The decision reason, which prefers the more right-skewed lottery, does not apply to either lottery. Since both lotteries are not right-skewed, the reason cannot be applied to this decision problem. Thus, the reason is not relevant in evaluating this choice.",
  "step_2": "The individual's verbal report states: 'Der Unterschied von 1000€ zwischen sicher 3000 und unsicheren 4000 ist mir zu gering.' This translates to: 'The difference of 1000€ between a certain 3000€ and an uncertain 4000€ is too small for me.' The individual is emphasizing the small difference in outcomes (3000€ vs. 4000€) and expresses that it is not worth the risk or uncertainty. The individual is focusing on the magnitude of the gain (1000€) and the certainty of the outcome in Lottery B. There is no mention of probabilities, skewness, unfavorable outcomes, or any consideration of the distribution of outcomes in terms of skewness. The deliberation centers on risk aversion and the perceived small value of a risky gain, not on the shape of the probability distribution (e.g., right-skewness).",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating skewness, specifically right-skewness — a greater portion of probability on unfavorable outcomes. In this case, both lotteries are not right-skewed: Lottery A is left-skewed (80% on 4000€), and Lottery B is certain (no unfavorable outcomes). Therefore, the condition for applying the reason is not met. The individual's justification is based on the small difference in monetary value and the certainty of the outcome, not on skewness. There is no reference to unfavorable outcomes or distributional shape. Since the decision reason is inapplicable to the lotteries and not referenced in the justification, the individual did not use the decision reason. The assessment is therefore certain that the reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
